    "answer": "Reported in Unspecified Interview Superday Market Knowledge Part 1: Yield Curve The yield curve plots interest rates of bonds with equal credit quality but different maturity dates. Normal (upward sloping): Longer-term rates higher than short-term. Signals healthy, growing economy. Inverted: Short-term rates higher than long-term. Often precedes recession (market expects rate cuts). Flat: Little difference between short and long-term rates. Signals economic uncertainty or transition. Part 2: Phillips Curve The Phillips curve describes an inverse relationship between unemployment and inflation: when unemployment is low, inflation tends to be high, and vice versa. Modern economics recognizes this relationship can break down (e.g., stagflation in 1970s), and central banks target both low unemployment and low inflation.",
